Write the **engine** for **Lattice**, a deterministic Factorio-style factory
simulation. You are not building an application and you are not writing a per-tick
callback — you are writing a function that takes a whole factory *scenario* (a
fixed tile layout plus a tick count and a snapshot schedule) and simulates it end
to end, emitting the factory's exact state at each scheduled snapshot. Compiled to
WebAssembly, your engine is run once per scenario and scored two ways, in order:
**first correctness, then fuel**.

Your project root is `{{workspace}}`. A complete starter is already in place
there; build your engine on top of it.

## How it works

A scenario is a static blueprint — belts, splitters, inserters, assemblers, and
the source/sink test fixtures — placed once on a tile grid, run for *N* ticks from
an empty start. Everything is integer / fixed-point: item positions, belt speeds,
swing and craft timers, all integers, so the state after *N* ticks is a single
well-defined value every correct engine must agree on to the bit.

Your engine is invoked **once per scenario**. It receives the `scenario` JSON and
must return the canonical `state` at each scheduled snapshot tick. Correctness is
checked bit-for-bit by **checksum** (Factorio's own desync-detection model): your
state must match the reference engine's at every snapshot or the scenario fails
outright — there is no partial credit on correctness. Only once your engine is
correct does its **fuel** — how much work it did — become its result. **Lower fuel
is better.**

## What to read first

The full specification is in the `specs/` directory at `{{workspace}}`, already
present in your project. Read all of it before you start, and produce an
implementation that matches the specification exactly.

## Your starting point

The workspace at `{{workspace}}` is a small Cargo project — just the engine crate.
Everything you build *against* and run *with* is provided by the run environment,
not vendored here:

- `engine/src/lib.rs` — the engine you fill in. It builds and runs as-is and
  produces *correct* output, but it is the FLOOR: a straightforward
  move-every-item-every-tick loop that spends far more fuel than it needs to.
  Replace its `run` with an efficient engine. You write a single
  `fn run(scenario: &Scenario) -> Vec<Snapshot>` and the `simulate!` macro wires
  it to the wasm ABI — see `specs/contract.md`.
- The contract types and the canonical state/checksum (`lattice-core` — the
  `Scenario`/`Snapshot` types, identical to what the harness scores you against)
  and the SDK (`lattice-sdk` — the ABI plumbing plus the re-exported types) are
  provided under `$LATTICE_HOME/buildkit` and are already wired up as your
  engine's path dependencies. You build against them; do not edit them. Note: the
  buildkit gives you the *types and the checksum*, not the rules engine — the
  simulation logic is yours to write from the specs.
- The `lattice` CLI is **preinstalled** on your `PATH`. It is both the **oracle**
  (`lattice solve` produces the expected `state` for any scenario) and the
  **local scorer** (`lattice run` builds/loads your module, checks it against the
  reference, and reports correct/incorrect + fuel using the same host the
  validator uses). `lattice gen` makes fresh scenarios; `lattice schema` dumps the
  schemas. See `specs/cli.md`.
- A set of **training scenarios** with their expected outputs lives under
  `$LATTICE_HOME/training/` — each a `scenario.json` paired with the reference's
  `expected.json`. They span the entity set and the tricky behaviors (a single
  side-loaded lane, a backed-up inserter, a saturated splitter, a starved-then-
  flooded assembler) so you can confirm your engine bit-for-bit. They are
  **practice, not the scored set**: the scenarios you are graded on are held out
  and unseen, deliberately larger and longer — there is no shortcut around
  actually simulating. (`$LATTICE_HOME` is `/opt/lattice`.)

## How to build

Your engine compiles to a `wasm32-unknown-unknown` **core module**:

```bash
cargo build --release --target wasm32-unknown-unknown -p engine
```

This emits `target/wasm32-unknown-unknown/release/engine.wasm` — the exact
artifact the harness loads and scores. Keep the crate named `engine` with
crate-type `cdylib` so that path is stable. The wasm target is already installed.

## What to deliver

A **correct** engine that spends **as little fuel as possible**. Correctness comes
first and is absolute — match the reference's checksums on every snapshot — but
correctness is the gate, not the goal: between two correct engines, the one that
does less work wins. The decisive move is the representation. A naive engine
re-walks every item on every belt every tick, most of that work re-confirming that
a long, already-compressed run did not change; the efficient engine Factorio
itself uses stores the **gaps between items** in each maximal straight run of belt
as a single transport line, advances a packed run in constant time, caches where
the last open gap is, and drives inserters/assemblers/sources/sinks by event
rather than polling. Same checksums, a fraction of the fuel — the transport-line
representation is the win.

Iterate locally with the preinstalled `lattice` CLI: `lattice run --module
target/wasm32-unknown-unknown/release/engine.wasm --scenario <s>` against the
training scenarios (and `lattice gen` larger ones) to confirm you are bit-exact and
to watch your fuel number, refining until it is both correct and lean. The CLI
prints `correct`/`INCORRECT at snapshot tick N (…)` so a divergence is easy to
locate, and the fuel it reports is the same number the scored run will record.
Stay inside the per-scenario fuel and memory limits in `specs/sandbox` (the
`[sandbox]` table; see `specs/contract.md`) — exceeding either fails that
scenario. When `cargo build --release --target wasm32-unknown-unknown -p engine`
produces an `engine.wasm` that is correct on the training set for a low fuel
number, you are done; the module is your submission.

## Commit your work

Your project root is a **git repository** — it was initialized before you
started, with the starter crate in its first commit. The wasm module is your
scored submission, but the **source you wrote is published as this run's public
repository**, so work you leave uncommitted is not published. When your engine is
correct and lean, commit everything: run `git add -A` then `git commit`, and
confirm `git status` reports a clean working tree with nothing uncommitted or
untracked. The `.gitignore` already excludes `/target/` (the harness rebuilds the
engine from source), so a clean tree does not mean committing build output — but
it does mean committing your `engine/` sources, `Cargo.toml`, and `Cargo.lock`.
